SELF STUDY QUESTIONS
1. What are SMART goals? Explain the need to which each responds.

   Answer: Specific - What? Who? How? The more precise your goal is, the more likely you will work towards it and feel able to know what to do.
   
   Measurable - A quantifiable target will help you track your progress and celebrate milestones.
   
   Attainable - If your goal is achievable, you will be more motivated to work towards it. Working on attainability also helps you identify gaps between what you hope to and can achieve.
   
   Relevant - Ensuring your goal relates to your overarching mission or value is essential to #purpose. Thinking about how your goal addresses an actual problem is essential!
   
   Time-bound - Having a team be on the same page for a deadline will help create accountability.

2. Name one overarching advantage and pitfall to implementation as a strategy for setting goals.

   Answer: Possible advantages:
   
   - It creates clear parameters for success that everyone can agree on. This makes you able to hold others accountable.
   - It motivates people to track their goals and report progress since reporting on SMART goals is easier.
   - It works like a spell-check for a goal -> to test if it is adequately phrased. It might be useful to apply once you already have an idea for a goal but to phrase it in a way that makes it precise and creates accountability.
   
   Possible disadvantages:
   
   - Setting goals too low. Aiming for attainability might reduce creativity and encourage people to set easy goals instead of working hard to try and reach challenging goals.
   - Some goals might not fit the SMART format or will change fundamentally when doing so. Imagine you want to make an organization more diverse because doing so improves social equity and the output of your organization. Quantifying how many people of specific backgrounds you want within your organization can make you aim low. More importantly, you might look past crucial aspects of diversity, such as creating an inclusive/accessible environment and addressing discrimination.
   - Merely focusing on short-term goals and losing track of values. Long-term goals and values can sometimes be too expansive to guide concrete next steps, but such goals are still valuable to expand our imagination about what’s possible. Wanting to make extraterrestrial life possible on Mars might not be very SMART, but it inspires people at SpaceX and can lead to many overspill innovations.

3. What principle of #scienceoflearning also applies (to an extent) to a goal that generates the most effort?

   Answer: Desirable Difficulty. Research shows that effort is highest when goals are challenging to achieve, as long as they don’t exceed one’s current capabilities by too much. Note: in #scienceoflearning, this effort is what ensures deep processing. In #purpose, it is what supports high performance and the ability to reach goals.

4. What are the four mechanisms that help people achieve goals? Tip: read the first resource in the Key Resources section. 

   Answer: Directive - they direct attention to goal-related activity and away from irrelevant activity
   
   Energizing - they inspire effort if motivational.
   
   Persistence - they keep people on track for a more extended period.
   
   Activate relevant knowledge/skills - goals contain language that directs people to the tools they already have to tackle those goals.

